156 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 116 0 R
/T (The DNS \(Domain Name System\) translates domain names into IP addresses, enabling not just IoT devices but other clients, like web browsers, email clients, servers, data centers as well to communicate with cloud services. However, conventional DNS does not inherently authenticate such responses, making the users vulnerable to DNS spoofing and response manipulation. Here is where DNSSEC \(Domain Name System Security Extensions\) comes into play, which addresses these issues by using digital signatures and a chain of trust to authenticate DNS data and detect invalid responses.)
/E (The DNS \(Domain Name System\) translates domain names into IP addresses, enabling not just IoT devices but other clients, like web browsers, email clients, servers, data centers as well to communicate with cloud services. However, conventional DNS does not inherently authenticate such responses, making the users vulnerable to DNS spoofing and response manipulation. Here is where DNSSEC \(Domain Name System Security Extensions\) comes into play, which addresses these issues by using digital signatures and a chain of trust to authenticate DNS data and detect invalid responses.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
157 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 116 0 R
/T (However, implementing DNSSEC validation directly on resource-constrained IoT devices can introduce much more computational and memory overhead than required. Here is where gateway-assisted DNSSEC offers an optimisation, by delegating the validation to a gateway while allowing the client to communicate with it through an authenticated, encrypted connection using TLP \(Transport Layer Protocol\). Thus, this approach aims to reduce client-side processing requirements while preserving DNS validation outcomes, as necessary.)
/E (However, implementing DNSSEC validation directly on resource-constrained IoT devices can introduce much more computational and memory overhead than required. Here is where gateway-assisted DNSSEC offers an optimisation, by delegating the validation to a gateway while allowing the client to communicate with it through an authenticated, encrypted connection using TLP \(Transport Layer Protocol\). Thus, this approach aims to reduce client-side processing requirements while preserving DNS validation outcomes, as necessary.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
